### 4 · Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Test(s) | Effort | Why first |
|----------|-------|---------|--------|-----------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` | Low | False-pass — zero defect detection value |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` | Low | Hidden dead branch — one scenario is untested |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` | Medium | Flaky CI failures + 2 s wasted per run |
| 4 | Mystery Guest + Resource Optimism | `ImportOrders_FromCsv_ParsesCorrectly` | Low | Guaranteed failure on any other machine |
| 5 | Exception Handling | `ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError` | Low | Quick 1-line swap to `Assert.ThrowsException` |
| 6 | Magic Number + Assertion Roulette | `CalculateTotal_ReturnsCorrectAmount` | Medium | Readability + diagnosability |
| 7 | Sensitive Equality | `GetOrderSummary_ReturnsFormattedString` | Medium | Brittle to formatting changes |
| 8 | Eager Test | `OrderProcessor_FullWorkflow_Succeeds` | High | May need split into multiple tests or integration marker |
| 9 | General Fixture | Class-level `_logger` | Low | Remove unused field |

**Bottom line:** 8 of 9 test methods carry at least one cataloged smell. The suite gives false confidence (assertion-free test), has a permanently dead branch (conditional logic), is environment-coupled (mystery guest), and is non-deterministic (sleepy test). Priorities 1–5 are quick wins that would dramatically improve reliability.
